{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_dhhuh67yegr36dx2e22e4pq2ujnn7cy5rl76anbfiak4onquehdq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. `c_src/` is untouched (only the `build/` directory was added, as instructed).\n\n## Result\n\n**The Rust translation was already correct.** I found no behavioural divergence from the C — but I only established that after making the test harness capable of *detecting* divergence, which took several rounds.\n\n### What was built\n\n| Artifact | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 3 C symbols (`get_os_arch`, `w_regexec`, `parse_uname_string`), all exported by Rust. `nm -D` diff **empty**. Verified `regoff_t`=4B, `regex_t`=64B via a compiled probe. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | **30 rows**, grepped from every `return`, guard and unchecked write in `lib.c` |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | **35 rows** over 12 branch axes the C actually distinguishes |\n| `tests/` | **74 tests**, all loading *both* `.so`s via `libloading` |\n\n### Three real bugs — all in my harness, not the Rust\n\n1. **stderr capture raced across test threads** — `dup2(2)` is process-global, so `regcomp` diagnostics leaked between parallel tests. Fixed with a global lock.\n2. **My E19 expectation was wrong.** I assumed `\"host [Ubuntu: 22.04 (\"` gives an empty codename. It gives *none*: `lib.c:106` chops the trailing byte **before** `lib.c:109` searches for `\" (\"`. I confirmed against the C library before changing the test — the C was right, I was wrong.\n3. **`malloc_usable_size` produced a false positive.** A 7-byte `strdup` reported usable 40, which is impossible for a fresh chunk — glibc had reused a 48-byte free chunk. Since C and Rust run back-to-back their heap histories differ, so in-process size comparison is invalid. I moved it to `phase_d_alloc_sizes.rs`, which measures each side in its **own subprocess** performing an identical allocation sequence.\n\n### Why I trust the green result\n\nPassing tests prove nothing unless they can fail, so `scripts/mutation_check.py` injects **37 deliberate bugs** into `src/lib.rs` and requires the suite to fail: `caught=37 survived=0`.\n\nThe first run had **1 survivor** — `malloc(match_size + 2)`. That is a genuine observable difference (the C documents that the caller must free these buffers), and closing it is what produced the subprocess allocation test above.\n\nTwo mutants are recorded as **provably equivalent and required to survive** — if either is ever caught, my equivalence argument is wrong and the suite is over-constrained. Rather than assert equivalence, `tests/equivalence_proofs.rs` demonstrates it against the real C library (e.g. a match always overwrites both `pmatch` slots, so the array's initializer is dead in the C too).\n\n`tests/soak.rs` at `SOAK_ITERS=500000` gave ≈1.5M randomized comparisons in release mode with zero divergences.\n\n### Two caveats worth flagging\n\n- **`Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`**, so \"every feature combination\" is one configuration. I compensated by running the full suite in **debug *and* release** — release differs materially (`panic = \"abort\"`, `overflow-checks = false`), which matters for the wrapping pointer arithmetic.\n- **`E30` and part of `E29` are documented, not executed.** `regexec` with `nmatch > 0` and `pmatch == NULL`, and `strstr(NULL, …)`, are UB with no C guard. Both libraries call the *same* libc with the same arguments, so they are identical by construction; `e29_null_uname_both_fault` asserts this out-of-process by comparing fatal signals. I did not fabricate in-process tests for these.\n\n`bash scripts/verify_gate.sh` re-checks every gate item, including that each table row names a test that actually exists — run from a clean `target/` it reports **VERIFICATION COMPLETE**."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1309,"cache_read_input_tokens":202612,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1309,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"22b379f9-876d-41fe-b19f-bdcb2c5959be","uuid":"ee2763f1-0f96-419b-b6f1-269a28a63c5b","timestamp":"2026-08-27T13:56:15.632Z"}
